description.narrative:
The objective of the intervention is to facilitate an improved business environment through strategic investment climate reform and private sector development.
The IFC Investment Climate Reform and Private Sector Development program successfully achieved key institutional, structural and legal reforms. Below are several tangible reforms that the intervention contributed to:  
1. drafted the Special Economic Zone Legislation and it was passed into law in October 2017;
2. the Insolvency and Restructuring Law passed successfully in late 2016;
3. drafting and the passage of the leasing law and a follow-up leasing forum that established the 1st leasing company in Liberia (M&E Leasing);
4. the implementation of new Commercial Code to address modern commercial transactions;
5. the creation of the Commercial Court, which has reduced the time it takes to resolve commercial disputes from 9 years to a matter of a few months for complex commercial cases;
6. administrative regulations removing the sanctioning powers from field inspectors, and creating an internal, transparent, and administrative review process for violating businesses;
7. administrative regulations reducing the frequency in which Ministry of Commerce inspectors conducted field inspections on businesses from almost daily to once per quarter for announced inspections;
8. a public-private dialogue platform was supported; the Liberia Better Business Forum (LBBF); through this forum positive legislative and institutional reforms came about;
9. the establishment of the business registry; making it easier for businesses to meet legal requirements and get permit to operate in Liberia; and
10. the movable collateral registry was set up; thus, providing additional avenues for business to use their assets to have access to finance.
